ly. So no vectorization possible there.\n\nCould use SIMD to compute distances for 4 points at once and then process them in sequence inside the same iteration of an outer loop that iterates over groups of 4? That would reduce memory traffic by not storing dists array. Let's consider: In each thread, we could have a local buffer of e.g., 8 floats to hold distances computed via NEON for 8 points, then process them through heap one by one. This avoids writing and reading the global dists array, which is beneficial if total is large (memory bandwidth). But current code already writes dists once and reads once; that's two memory accesses per point plus xs/ys reads. Fusing would reduce to just xs/ys reads and no dists store/load. That could be significant.\n\nWe can implement a fused loop: each thread processes its chunk in blocks of 4 (or 8) using NEON intrinsics to compute distances, then for each lane extract distance and index, update heap. Need constant lane indices; we can use vgetq_lane_f32 with constants 0..3. That is legal.\n\nBut previous attempt failed because they used variable lane index in a loop over lanes? We'll write explicit unrolled code with constant indices.\n\nAlso need to handle remainder points (total not multiple of block size). We can process the bulk with SIMD and tail scalar.\n\nWe must ensure tie-breaking: when distances equal, smaller index preferred. The heap uses is_worse(da, ia, db, ib) returns true if da > db or (da==db && ia>ib), meaning a is worse than b if larger distance or same distance but larger index. So root is worst among current k. When replacing, we replace root with new point only if new point is better (i.e., not worse than root). That preserves smallest indices for equal distances.\n\nIn fused loop, we process points in increasing index order within each thread's chunk? The static schedule assigns contiguous chunks to threads; within a thread, the loop iterates p from start to end-1. So indices are processed in ascending order. For equal distances, later (larger) indices will be considered worse and not replace earlier ones if heap already contains smaller index with same distance. That matches original sequential behavior? The original baseline likely processes points sequentially and maintains top-k; when a new point has equal distance to an existing one, it replaces only if its index is smaller? Actually the standard algorithm: for each point in order, if dist < max_dist or (dist == max_dist && idx < max_idx) then replace. But here they use heap with root being worst (largest distance, tie larger index). When new point d,p comes, if it's better than root (i.e., not worse), we replace root. So for equal distances, a later point with same distance but larger index is considered worse and will NOT replace an existing smaller-index point already in heap. That preserves earliest indices. Good.\n\nIn fused loop, processing ascending p ensures that when we encounter a point with same distance as some earlier one, the earlier one (smaller index) is already in heap if it was among top-k; later larger index won't displace it unless there are fewer than k points or all existing have worse distances. So correct.\n\nNow implement fused loop per thread:\n\nWe'll keep the parallel region but replace two loops with a single loop that computes distances and updates heap for each point, using NEON to compute 4 at a time. However, we need to ensure SIMD is used; we can use `#pragma omp simd`? No because of heap dependency. We'll manually vectorize distance computation.\n\nWe need to include arm_neon.h (since architecture ARM64). The compiler flags likely allow NEON intrinsics. Previous build failed due to lane index constant issue, but we'll fix that.\n\nLet's design:\n\nInside parallel region:\n- Each thread gets its chunk start and end based on static schedule? We can use `#pragma omp for` with a loop over blocks of 4, but then each iteration processes 4 points; the heap update is sequential within iteration. That would still be vectorized for distance computation if we compute all four distances using NEON before updating heap.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n```c\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n    float *hd = local_d + (size_t)tid * per;\n    long *hi = local_i + (size_t)tid * per;\n    char *hl = local_l + (size_t)tid * per;\n    int hn = 0;\n\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n    for (long p0 = 0; p0 < (long)total; p0 += 4) {\n        long nrem = total - p0;\n        if (nrem >= 4) {\n            float32x4_t dxv = vsubq_f32(vld1q_f32(&xs[p0]), vdupq_n_f32(tx));\n            float32x4_t dyv = vsubq_f32(vld1q_f32(&ys[p0]), vdupq_n_f32(ty));\n            float32x4_t dv = vfmaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dxv, dxv), dyv, dyv);\n            // extract lanes\n            float d[4];\n            vst1q_f32(d, dv); // store to array; or use vgetq_lane with constants\n            for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {\n                long p = p0 + j;\n                if (hn < k) heap_push(...);\n                else if (is_worse(hd[0], hi[0], d[j], p)) heap_replace_root(...);\n            }\n        } else {\n            for (long p = p0; p < total; ++p) { ... scalar ...}\n        }\n    }\n    local_n[tid] = hn;\n}\n```\n\nBut note: `#pragma omp for` with stride 4 will distribute blocks of 4 to threads, but the loop variable p0 increments by 4. That's fine. However, we need to ensure that each thread processes contiguous points? Static schedule divides iterations (blocks) among threads; since block size is 4, each thread gets a set of non-contiguous blocks if total not multiple of num_threads*4? Actually static schedule with default chunk will assign contiguous chunks of iterations (each iteration being one p0 value). So each thread gets a contiguous range of p0 values, which corresponds to contiguous points. Good.\n\nBut we must be careful: the loop condition `p0 < total` and step 4; if total not multiple of 4, last block may have fewer than 4; handle remaind